<section class="wrap section" id="how">
<h2>How to install it</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Download</strong><span>On your phone, tap the download button above. Your phone may warn that this type of file can harm your device. That appears for every app installed this way. Tap <b>Download anyway</b> if you're happy to continue.</span></li>
<li><strong>Open the file</strong><span>When the download finishes, tap <b>Open</b>, or open the file from your Downloads folder.</span></li>
<li><strong>Allow it</strong><span>Android will say it can't install apps from this source. Tap <b>Settings</b>, then switch on <b>Allow from this source</b> for the app you downloaded it with, such as Chrome or Files. Go back and tap <b>Install</b>.</span></li>
<li><strong>Open Deaftly</strong><span>When it says the app is installed, tap <b>Open</b>.</span></li>
<li><strong>Tidy up</strong><span>You can now switch Allow from this source off again. Doing so doesn't affect Deaftly.</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="muted">Menu names differ between phone makers. If you can't see the option, search your phone's Settings for “Install unknown apps”.</p>

<h3>Updating</h3>
<p>Download the new version from this page, open it and tap <b>Update</b> or <b>Install</b>. It installs over the old one.</p>
<p>If Android says the app conflicts with an existing one, or that the signatures don't match, stop. The file didn't come from Deaftly. Don't uninstall your copy to get around it: download it again from this page.</p>

<details class="panel">
<summary>For techies: check the signature</summary>
<p>Every official release is signed with this SHA-256 certificate fingerprint:</p>
<code class="fingerprint">CD:70:A2:F4:8E:E6:1F:8D:90:31:91:CE:BF:D8:D0:D7:2B:D2:57:C9:11:D8:DC:10:0F:A6:FE:AD:F7:6A:53:EB</code>
<p>Check it with <code>apksigner verify --print-certs</code> from the Android SDK build tools.</p>
</details>
